WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.801
Safe binary parsing is explicit.

2
00:00:01.801 --> 00:00:09.727
A parser should avoid directly casting a byte buffer into a C++ struct and treating the result as a valid protocol object.

3
00:00:09.727 --> 00:00:17.653
That shortcut can depend on compiler layout, padding, alignment, object lifetime, and platform details that are not necessarily part of the protocol.

4
00:00:17.653 --> 00:00:20.535
It may also hide where validation should occur.

5
00:00:20.535 --> 00:00:22.697
A reviewable parser reads fields deliberately.

6
00:00:22.697 --> 00:00:27.741
Before reading each field, it checks that enough bytes remain inside the current frame.

7
00:00:27.741 --> 00:00:37.107
It advances a cursor in a visible way, converts raw bytes into fixed-width values with known endianness, and validates the result before using it for

8
00:00:37.107 --> 00:00:39.269
offsets, counts, allocation, or state changes.

9
00:00:39.269 --> 00:00:45.033
If a helper reads from the cursor, the helper should make failure and cursor movement obvious.

10
00:00:45.033 --> 00:00:48.996
Separating raw input from trusted parsed objects keeps the design clearer.

11
00:00:48.996 --> 00:00:50.437
Raw bytes remain untrusted.

12
00:00:50.437 --> 00:00:52.239
Parsed fields are intermediate values.

13
00:00:52.239 --> 00:00:58.723
Trusted application objects are created only after required fields, lengths, ranges, versions, and state rules have been checked.

14
00:00:58.723 --> 00:01:04.488
This separation prevents partially parsed data from leaking into business logic as if it were complete.

15
00:01:04.488 --> 00:01:07.370
Offsets and derived ranges need the same discipline.

16
00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:16.016
A parser should prove that offset plus size stays within the current message, that nested records do not overlap unexpectedly, and that the cursor

17
00:01:16.016 --> 00:01:17.818
cannot move past the boundary.

18
00:01:17.818 --> 00:01:23.222
Hidden pointer arithmetic, unchecked indexing, and implicit cursor mutation make those proofs hard to review.

19
00:01:23.222 --> 00:01:26.824
The best parsing code is often plain rather than clever.

20
00:01:26.824 --> 00:01:31.868
Each read says what it needs, what it produced, and what validation happened next.

21
00:01:31.868 --> 00:01:34.750
That makes binary protocol behavior testable and maintainable.

22
00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:43.036
It also gives reviewers a direct path through the code: frame validation, field extraction, range checks, state checks, and finally construction of a

23
00:01:43.036 --> 00:01:45.918
trusted object that application logic may use safely.

24
00:01:45.918 --> 00:01:48.080
Boring code is a strength here.
